TI Rapid training of neural networks as accelerated, differentiable surrogates of kinetic Monte Carlo polymerization models
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Polymerization kinetics; Kinetic Monte Carlo
ID FREE-RADICAL POLYMERIZATION; METHYL-METHACRYLATE; SIMULATION; STYRENE; COPOLYMERIZATION; ALGORITHMS; MECHANISM
AB Kinetic Monte Carlo (kMC) simulations are a useful tool for kinetic modelling of many (bio)chemical processes. However, particularly for the case of polymerization reactions, which require relatively large simulation volumes to ensure statistical validity and often involve complex operations, they are computationally expensive to run. In this work, an approach to rapidly generate neural network-based surrogate models from first principles kinetic Monte Carlo simulations is explored. Training datasets of noisy, but statistically representative, low simulation volume kMC simulations of solution polymerization processes are generated for subsequent training of neural network models capable of prediction of a range of macromolecular properties of interest, including molar mass distributions. Crucially, despite being trained on noisy data, the neural network models are shown to be capable of generating data of comparable quality to high-fidelity kinetic Monte Carlo simulations. In addition to the speed with which they can be trained, it is shown that the differentiable nature of neural networks allows the trained models to be used for rapid parameter optimization and for recipe design for specific macromolecular targets. This opens the way for making use of kinetic Monte Carlo simulations in areas where computational constraints have previously restricted their use.
