TI An energy-based deep learning method for fiber-reinforced elastomers based on gradient elasticity theory: theoretical model, numerical simulation and experiments
SO THIN-WALLED STRUCTURES
LA English
DT Article
DE Physics-informed neural networks; Deep energy method; Gradient elasticity; Fiber reinforced materials; Digital image correlation
ID HYPERELASTIC CONSTITUTIVE MODEL; MECHANICAL RESPONSE; RESISTANT; NETWORKS
AB By introducing second-order deformation gradients, gradient elasticity theory effectively accounts for the additional fiber flexural resistance in fiber-reinforced elastomers within the hyperelastic regime. However, the inclusion of higher-order kinematic descriptions increases the order of the governing equations, thereby significantly complicating numerical solution procedures. This paper proposes an energy-based physics-informed neural network framework for efficiently solving fiber-reinforced elastomer models that incorporate second-gradient terms. The framework employs a dual-branch neural network architecture that simultaneously approximates the displacement field and its first-order gradient, while second-order terms are computed via automatic differentiation. By decoupling the network predictions, this meshfree approach achieves stable solutions effectively mitigating numerical instability issues. To validate the accuracy and robustness of the proposed method, a series of experiments on fiber-reinforced rubber specimens was designed and conducted to systematically examine the effects of fiber stiffness and orientation symmetry on the deformation field. The full-field displacement results obtained through digital image correlation show excellent agreement with the numerical predictions. The results demonstrate that the developed neural network framework provides a reliable and efficient analytical tool for investigating higher-order deformation behaviors.
