DE RSC biomass; PLA plastics; Thermal degradation; Machine learning; Thermokinetics; Circular economy
ID HIGH-DENSITY POLYETHYLENE; CATALYTIC CO-PYROLYSIS; BIOMASS; BEHAVIORS; PLASTICS; GROUNDS; DECOMPOSITION; POLYPROPYLENE; EQUILIBRIUM; FIBER
AB The necessity of identifying a reliable, sustainable, and economical strategy for mitigating the ever-increasing volume of "mismanaged plastics" is of considerable interest to plastic manufacturers, petrochemical industries, environmental engineers, energy experts, and policymakers globally. This study presents, for the first time, a comprehensive analysis of the thermal degradation of Recycled Saudi Coffee (RSC) and polylactic acid (PLA) biocomposites, employing machine learning convolutional deep neural networks alongside thermo-kinetic analysis. This assessment was conducted using thermogravimetric (TGA) and derivative thermogravimetric (DTG) data obtained at heating rates of 20, 40, and 60 degrees C.min-1 over a degradation temperature range of 25-600 degrees C, complemented by Fourier Transform Infrared (FTIR) analysis of the materials. Analysis of the TGA data for the composites revealed a synergistic interaction between the RSC biomass and PLA plastic, which altered the degradation temperature at the propagation stage, reduced thermal stability with increasing RSC biomass content in the composites , identified the presence of lignin in the RSC biomass , composites that slows their degradation at elevated temperatures. Kinetic studies involving the application of Criado's master plots and Coats-Redfern (CR) methods indicated that the second-order reaction (F2) is the favoured solid- state reaction mechanism. Activation energy values ranging from 58.772 to 294.045 kJ mol-1,-1 , 92.262-122.019 kJ mol-1,-1 , 87.314-117.405 kJ mol-1,-1 , 87.704-117.844 kJ mol-1-1 were esti- mated using the Coats-Redfern (CR), Flynn-Wall-Ozawa (FWO), Kissinger-Akahira-Sunose (KAS), and Starink (STK) isoconvensional models, respectively. Deep learning modelling and simulation techniques, involving the combined utilization of convolutional neural networks (CNN) and deep neural networks (DNN), were employed to extract key features and labels, followed by the training of the extracted experimental data. This approach yielded predicted responses for the TGA traces and interpolated data curves that were consistent within the experimental variability. The methodology employed 198,516 epochs, incorporating a combined convolutional neural network (CNN) and two deep neural network (DNN) layers characterized by six hidden neurons, along with a sigmoid activation function. Notably, the analysis identified sample composition as the most sensitive parameter influencing the thermal degradation of these materials. The strategy employed in this study and the resulting data could provide valuable insights into the use of RSC biomass as a biocatalyst accelerator in plastic processing.
