TI Predictive modeling of seeded semi-batch emulsion polymerization of n-butyl acrylate using polymer chemistry-informed neural networks
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
ID MOLECULAR-WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION; POPULATION BALANCE-EQUATIONS; RADICAL POLYMERIZATION; PARTICLE; COPOLYMERIZATION; DISCRETIZATION; MONOMER
AB Predictive modeling of emulsion polymerization is challenging as it requires a mechanistic understanding not only of compartmentalized radical polymerization kinetics but also of the complex colloidal phenomena involved in the process. In order to make the problem more manageable, most models of emulsion polymerization are forced to make simplifying assumptions, which often leads to differences between the predictions of the model and experimental data. In addition, most kinetic models rely on uncertain or highly correlated parameters, making accurate prediction difficult. In this work, a polymer chemistry-informed neural network (PCINN) is presented to model a complex emulsion polymerization system, aiming to overcome the limitations of traditional approaches by combining first-principles knowledge in the form of a limited-fidelity kinetic model with a data-driven component. As a representative system of direct industrial relevance, the case of the seeded semibatch emulsion polymerization of n-butyl acrylate was considered. The predictive performance of the PCINN model was compared with that of a purely mechanistic model and a standard neural network and was shown to lead to substantially improved prediction accuracy. Furthermore, it was demonstrated that PCINNs can be used as on-the-fly models for fast, real-time predictions of this complex polymerization system.
