TI Structure stability optimization of polymer retarder using response surface methodology and prediction of retarding performance using artificial neural network at high-temperature
SO JOURNAL OF MOLECULAR STRUCTURE
LA English
DT Article
DE Oil well cement; Polymer retarder; High-temperature; Prediction; Neural network
ID ITACONIC ACID; COPOLYMER
AB Retarders with stable structures at high- temperature are crucial for ensuring pumping safe of cement slurry in ultra deep well cementing. The sensitivity of retarders at high-temperature and the limitations of hightemperature experimental results in low design efficiency of high-temperature cement slurry. This study developed a high-temperature retarder (HF-5L), using 2-acrylamido-2-methylpropanesulfonic acid (AMPS), itaconic acid (IA), N,N-dimethylacrylamide (DMAA), N-vinylpyrrolidone (NVP), and methacryloyl propyl trimethylammonium chloride (MAPTAC). The monomer ratio was optimized using response surface methodology (RSM), and the synergistic effects between multiple monomers were analyzed. The performance of HF-5L was tested, and an artificial neural network (ANN) model was established to predict the thickening time of cement slurry at extremely high temperatures and determine the optimal retarder dosage for various high-temperature conditions. Results showed that, the optimal ratio of DMAA:NVP:MAPTAC = 0.7:1:0.6, HF-5L maintains structural stability at 349 degrees C, suitable for ultra-high temperature of 240 degrees C. The thickening time of cement slurry exceeded 180 min at 200 degrees C with 2.5 % HF-5L. The 24-hour compressive strength of cement samples at 60 degrees C exceeded 14 MPa. The neural network model predicted a thickening time of 271.80 min at 240 degrees C with 3.5 % HF5L, showing a relative error of 17.13 % compared to the experimental value. It has higher prediction accuracy compared to regression models. The thickening time meeting construction requirements by minimizing the retarder dosage inversed from the prediction model, ensured construction efficiency. These findings offer technical support for the design of high-temperature retarders and provide an effective method for predicting the thickening time of cement slurry at high temperatures, improving the design efficiency of high-temperature cement slurry.
