TI Molecular descriptor-driven machine learning for predicting polymer glass transition temperature
SO POLYMER
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Polymers; Glass transition temperature
ID LANGUAGE
AB Understanding and controlling the thermal-mechanical properties of polymers hinges on accurately predicting the glass transition temperature (Tg). In this study, we employed three machine learning models - Deep Neural Network (DNN), Random Forest (RF), and Support Vector Machine (SVM) - to forecast the Tg of polymers. Two distinct approaches, Molecular Descriptor (MD) and Molecular Fingerprint (MF), were utilized to characterize polymer features. Statistical indicators of model performance revealed that the DNN-MD model exhibited optimal results on the test set, boasting an R2 of 0.913 and an RMSE of 25.582. Moreover, within the RF and SVM models, using MD as input for polymer features displayed superior performance. The SHAP analysis identified key features associated with high and low Tg values, offering robust guidance for designing novel polymer structures. This approach holds promise for accelerating the development of new materials, providing crucial support for advancements in polymer applications.
