TI Polyurethanes synthetized with polyols of distinct molar masses: Use of the artificial neural network for prediction of degree of polymerization
SO POLYMER ENGINEERING AND SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE artificial neural network; differential calorimetric analysis; molar mass; polyurethane
ID SOFT SEGMENT; CHEMICAL-STRUCTURE; MOLECULAR-WEIGHT; THERMOPLASTIC POLYURETHANES; MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; POLYCARBONATE DIOLS; REACTION-KINETICS; ELASTOMERS; MORPHOLOGY
AB The molar mass of the polyurethanes (PUs)' reagents directly influences their thermal response, affecting both the polymerization process and the enthalpy and the degree of reaction. This study reports applying an artificial neural network (ANN), associated with surface response methodology (SRM) models, to predict the calorimetric behavior of certain PU's bulk polymerizations. A noncatalyzed reaction between an aliphatic hexamethylene diisocyanate (HDI) and a polycarbonate diol (PCD) with distinct molar masses (500, 1000, and 2000 g/mol) was proposed. A high level of reliability of the predicted calorimetric curves was obtained due to an excellent agreement between theoretical and modeled results, enabling creating a 3D surface response to predict the reaction kinetics. Also, it was possible to observe that the polymerization kinetics is affected by the -OH group's association phenomena. The applied methodology can be extended for other materials or properties of interest.
