TI Physics-informed modeling of steady-state solvent mass transfer in polymer-containing ultrafiltration under agitation
SO SEPARATION AND PURIFICATION TECHNOLOGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Ultrafiltration of polymer-containing solutions; Steady-state mass transfer; Sherwood number; Agitated ultrafiltration; Physics-informed neural networks; Bayesian calibration
ID CONCENTRATION POLARIZATION; CROSS-FLOW
AB In ultrafiltration of polymer-containing solutions operated under steady-state conditions with agitation, mass transfer at the membrane-solution interface significantly influences permeate flux. However, available Sherwood correlations for stirred configurations describe transport behavior in terms of classical dimensionless groups without explicitly incorporating polymer concentration as an independent operational variable. In this work, a physically consistent modeling approach is developed and assessed for steady-state mass-transfer characterization in a poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA)-water ultrafiltration system using polyethersulfone (PES) membranes. An empirical mass-transfer correlation calibrated via Bayesian inference is established as the reference description. An artificial neural network (ANN) is employed exclusively as a data-driven benchmark for permeate-flux prediction, whereas a physics-informed lightweight neural network (PINN-lite) with physical anchoring is introduced as the main methodological contribution for Sherwood-number estimation. The results show that both the Sherwood number and the permeate flux exhibit systematic and reproducible dependencies on agitation intensity and polymer concentration, with the PINN-lite model providing the best overall predictive performance among the evaluated approaches. The proposed approach combines physical interpretability and predictive capability, providing a consistent basis for modeling and analysis within the investigated experimental domain.
