TI Integrated machine learning approach for final weight prediction in stimuli-responsive four-dimensional printed single-layer elastomer strip
SO ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Four-dimensional printing; Single-layer elastomer strip; Machine learning; Deep learning; Final weight prediction
ID POLYMER
AB This paper presents an integrated machine learning (ML) framework for predicting the final weight of four-dimensional printed (4DP) single-layer elastomer strips fabricated via material extrusion. Strips composed of Polyurethane (PU), Eudragit (R) S100, and Polyethylene Glycol 400 (PEG 400) were engineered to respond to pH and temperature through swelling and heat-induced transformations. Experimental analysis showed a strong correlation (r = 0.997) between the initial and final weights, with an initial weight contributing 98.85 % to the predictive accuracy and a thickness of only 0.03 %. Five ML models were evaluated using design attributes such as thickness, pH, swelling time, and initial weight. The Decision Tree (DT) achieved the highest accuracy (>99.95 %), followed by Random Forest (RF) and Extreme Gradient Boosting (XGBOOST) (>99.90 %), outperforming the Artificial Neural Network (ANN) (>98.30 %). The proposed framework highlights the superior performance of ensemble methods in capturing deterministic relationships, offering distinct advantages over physics-based models by combining computational efficiency with a high predictive accuracy.
