TI Estimation of finger joint angles based on polymer-embedded fiber Bragg grating sensor using machine learning
SO MEASUREMENT
LA English
DT Article
DE Wearable sensors; Polymer-embedded fiber Bragg grating sensor; Finger joints angles; Machine learning (ML)
ID STRAIN SENSOR
AB The wearable sensors offer effective means for monitoring human limb motion. However, accurately capturing finger joint angle variations during complex movements such as gripping and pinching remains a challenge. Optical fiber sensors are well suited for wearable applications due to their high stability, compact size, lightweight nature, and strong embeddability. In this study, we present a flexible wearable polymer-embedded fiber Bragg grating (FBG) sensor for estimating finger joint angles using machine learning. The sensor is fabricated by embedding an FBG into a polydimethylsiloxane (PDMS) elastomer (Sylgard 184, 10:1 base-to-curing agent ratio, cured at 70 degrees C). The experimental results show that the proposed sensor demonstrates high stability and multimodal sensing capability, with a pressure sensitivity of 3.53 pm/kg and a highly linear temperature response in the 30-40 degrees C range (R2 = 0.996). To evaluate its performance in motion tracking, we conduct experiments to estimate finger joint movement angles using the proposed sensor. A machine learning-based framework is developed, in which four models, including Support Vector Regression (SVR), Deep Neural Network (DNN), Recurrent Neural Network (RNN), and Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM), are employed and systematically compared. The experimental results indicate that the proposed framework achieves outstanding predictive accuracy for both discrete and continuous finger joint angles, attaining a maximum R2 of 0.9991. Overall, this study provides an effective and reliable approach for biomechanical motion monitoring and offers strong potential for integration into next-generation wearable health monitoring systems.
