AU Wu, DX
   Fan, HG
AF Wu, Dongxiao
   Fan, Haigui
TI Real-time tensile failure prediction of carbon fiber-reinforced polymer in high-pressure hydrogen storage vessels based on acoustic emission
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF HYDROGEN ENERGY
LA English
DT Article
DE Carbon fiber reinforced polymer; Ultimate bearing capacity prediction; Acoustic emission; Deep learning; Long short-term memory network
AB Carbon fiber-reinforced polymer (CFRP) materials are extensively used in high-pressure hydrogen storage vessels, but their ultimate bearing capacity is inherently uncertain. Therefore, real-time evaluation is essential to ensure operational safety. This study proposes a real-time prediction method for the ultimate bearing capacity of CFRP laminates using acoustic emission (AE) technology. A Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-based neural network is developed to fuse multiple AE features into a criterion function characterizing tensile damage evolution. Based on the framework, two prediction strategies are implemented for real-time prediction. Results show that although early-stage predictions show relatively large deviations, they gradually improve with accumulating AE data, ultimately converging to the actual ultimate bearing capacity. Finally, both strategies achieve high prediction accuracy, with relative errors consistently below 10 %, and minimum relative errors reaching 3.84 % and 2.80 % respectively. These findings confirm that the real-time prediction method holds significant potential for the online evaluation of hydrogen storage vessels.
