TI Model Predictive Control of PVC Polymerization Temperature Based on the Koopman Operator Extracted From Feedforward Neural Network
SO IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON AUTOMATION SCIENCE AND ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Water heating; Predictive control; Heat transfer; Inductors; Prediction algorithms; Mathematical models; Temperature; Predictive models; Cooling; Koopman operator; model predictive control; polyvinyl chloride
ID REACTORS
AB Due to the fact that the Polyvinyl Chloride (PVC) polymerization process is a time-varying, nonlinear process with large time delays, there are many uncertainties and external disturbances during production, making it difficult to establish precise mathematical models. Traditional control methods often fail to meet the control requirements under such circumstances. Therefore, from a data-driven perspective, a Model Predictive Control (MPC) method for PVC polymerization temperature based on the Koopman Operator and Feedforward Neural Networks has been proposed. This method first uses a Feedforward Neural Network(FNN) to extract the Koopman Operator from the temperature data of the PVC polymerization process, thereby constructing the state-space equation of the PVC polymerization process. Finally, it employs MPC to control the temperature of the PVC polymerization process. This method does not require prior knowledge or a mechanistic model of the system, achieving data-driven modeling and control. Simulation results have confirmed the effectiveness of this method in controlling the temperature of the PVC polymerization reaction. Note to Practitioners-This study is motivated by the need to develop an efficient temperature control algorithm for the polyvinyl chloride (PVC) production process to meet production requirements. Existing control algorithms typically have various limitations when applied to such complex nonlinear chemical processes, such as poor control accuracy or significantly increased computational overhead to ensure accuracy. These limitations may lead to a decline in the quality of PVC products. To solve these problems, this work proposes a data-driven control method that avoids the computational overhead associated with handling nonlinear issues and improves control accuracy. The effectiveness of this approach has been verified through simulation experiments.
