TI Facilitating small-data deep learning for predicting polymer mechanical properties using complex discrete networks via community detection algorithms
SO ENGINEERING FRACTURE MECHANICS
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer networks; Community detection; Transfer learning; Small-data deep learning; Mechanical property prediction
ID FRACTURE
AB Small-data prediction of elastic moduli and crack paths in networked soft materials is valuable for engineering design when labelled data are costly to obtain. Convolutional neural networks (CNNs) learn most efficiently from spatially coherent patterns, whereas raw discrete-network images are sparse, high-frequency line graphs in which information is concentrated in few pixels and governed by nonlocal connectivity. We address this mismatch by combining community detection (CD) with transfer learning, converting each network into a community density map that reduces high-frequency sparsity while preserving mesoscale organization. Unlike simple Gaussian blurring or local density smoothing, the community representation reflects an emergent structural scale of the network, capturing strongly connected groups that act as functional units. With 100 samples, this representation reduces modulus-prediction error by 45.57%, achieving an MAE of 11.62 +/- 6.88 relative to raw images. For crack trajectories, it achieves a mean SSIM of 0.87 and a mean IoU of 0.59, indicating that the predicted paths closely follow the ground-truth traces and recover most of the crack region overlap. Limitations include reliance on simulation-generated two-dimensional networks and sensitivity to community-resolution choices; future work will pursue adaptive resolution selection and extension to more realistic defects and three-dimensional networks.
