AU Shah, K
   Acar, P
   Seidel, GD
AF Shah, Kavan
   Acar, Pinar
   Seidel, Gary D.
TI Data-driven machine learning model for predicting multi-functional properties of CNT-polymer nanocomposites using conductivity-informed correlation functions
SO COMPUTATIONAL MATERIALS SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE Carbon nanotube; Piezoresistivity; Electrical percolation; Descriptive statistics; Machine learning; Reduced-order model; Multi-functional materials
ID ELECTRICAL-CONDUCTIVITY; CARBON; LINKAGES; PIEZORESISTIVITY; DEFORMATION; COMPOSITES; KNOWLEDGE
AB In this study, reduced-order structure-property relations are established to predict the electrical conductivity and piezoresistivity of Carbon Nanotube (CNT)-polymer nanocomposites whose microstructure is represented via statistical volume elements (SVEs). The SVEs are statistically characterized using two-point cluster functions and two-point blocking functions to capture information about the connectedness of CNTs. In addition, the auto-correlations, two-point cluster functions, and two-point blocking functions of the binarized conductivity contours associated with the SVEs are constructed to capture the conductivity enhancement of the polymer due to the presence of CNTs. Each of these two-point functions is represented in a lower-dimensional space using Principal Component Analysis (PCA). Structure-property linkages are established using Artificial Neural Networks (ANNs) that predict the conductivity and piezoresistivity of the SVEs using these PC scores as input. It is found that the auto-correlations of the binarized conductivity contours constitute the most predictive feature set for the conductivity and piezoresistivity. Furthermore, the two-point cluster and blocking functions fail to reveal additional predictive information about the properties over their auto-correlation counterparts.
