TI Intelligent prediction model of a polymer fracture grouting effect based on an information acquisition optimizer-optimized random forest
SO RESULTS IN ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer grouting; Prediction model; Random forest; Fracture network
AB During underground engineering activities such as mining and tunnel excavation, the excavation-induced exposure of rock fracture networks can trigger geological hazards, including collapses and groundwater inflow, potentially resulting in significant casualties and economic losses. Foamed polymer materials, known for their high expansion ratios, excellent impermeability, and environmentally friendly properties, have been extensively used in grouting and remediation projects. However, accurately determining the final sealing area ratio and water blocking rate during practical grouting operations remains highly challenging. Therefore, early prediction of grouting performance in dynamic water-bearing fracture networks is essential for ensuring construction safety and efficiency. This study introduces a predictive model based on a Random Forest algorithm optimized by the Information Acquisition Optimization technique. The model predicts the diffusion and sealing performance of foamed polymers, with key performance metrics including the water blocking rate and sealing area ratio. To support the model development, a detachable experimental apparatus was designed for dynamic water grouting tests in fracture networks. The effects of fracture network characteristics and grouting pressure under dynamic water conditions were systematically examined. The Information Acquisition Optimization -optimized Random Forest model outperforms traditional Backpropagation Neural Network and Particle Swarm Optimization-Based Backpropagation Neural Network models, achieving superior prediction accuracy, with improved R2 values for both water blocking rate and sealing area ratio. This study provides practical insights for enhancing grouting construction in underground engineering projects.
