TI Press-to-solder: Dynamic supramolecular soldering enabled by a deep eutectic polymer/liquid metal composite for robust soft-rigid electronics integration
SO MATERIALS TODAY CHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Article
DE Press-to-Solder; Supramolecular deep eutectic polymer; Liquid metal; Stretchable electronics; Chip integration
ID CONDUCTORS
AB The inherently weak interfacial adhesion and stress concentration at soft-rigid junctions are fundamental bottlenecks for stretchable electronics. Here, we address this by introducing a "Press-to-Solder" paradigm, realized through a rationally designed dynamic supramolecular deep eutectic polymer (PDES)-liquid metal (LM) composite. The key innovation lies in the use of choline chloride as a competitive hydrogen-bonding modulator within a choline dihydrogen citrate/acrylic acid network. This selectively decouples the network's energydissipating capacity from its interfacial adhesion, creating a "molecular solder" with high stretchability (similar to 2100%) and a lap-shear strength of similar to 1.27 MPa for the optimized composition. Crucially, under gentle pressure, this abundance of dynamic bonds can penetrate the LM oxide layer, inducing its rupture and stabilizing the percolating conductive network, achieving a metal-grade conductivity of 3.5 x 10(5) S m(-1) and an ultra-low contact resistance of 0.21 Omega. This pressure-enabled, room-temperature "soldering" mechanism instantly forms robust electrical and mechanical connections that remain intact under severe deformation. We further showcase its versatility in a breathing-lamp circuit, chip-integrated systems, and on-skin electromyography sensors, achieving 82.68% gesture recognition accuracy via a 1D convolutional neural network. This work presents a molecularly engineered platform unifying metal-grade conductivity, extreme stretchability, and instant chip integration for wearable electronics.
