TI LIGNIN HYDROGEL-A COMPLEX ORGANIC POLYMER DERIVED FROM BLACK LIQUOR FOR SUSTAINABLE AGRICULTURE
SO BULLETIN OF THE CHEMICAL SOCIETY OF ETHIOPIA
LA English
DT Article
DE KEY WORDS; Nano-lignin hydrogel; Black liquor valorization; Water retention; Controlled nutrient release; Machine learning optimization
AB The present work reports the synthesis of nano-lignin-based hydrogel derived from industrial black liquor, aimed at enhancing soil moisture retention and controlled urea release. Lignin nanoparticles were synthesized by acid precipitation followed by sonication, and subsequently incorporated into sodium hydroxide-urea polymeric matrix to form a hydrogel. The Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy, X-ray diffraction, and field-emission scanning electron microscopy were used to characterize the structure and morphology of the synthesized hydrogel. The hydrogel exhibited a water retention capacity of 68.3% within 48 hours, while 74.2% of urea was released over 120 hours, confirming its effective slow-release behavior as observed through experimental analysis. Machine learning techniques were employed using a dataset comprising 100 experimentally synthesized samples and their corresponding variable parameters to evaluate hydrogel performance. Among the tested models, the Artificial Neural Network revealed higher predictive accuracy, achieving C = 0.982, RMSE = 0.038, and MAE = 0.021 for water retention, and R = 0.967, RMSE = 0.044, and MAE = 0.027 for urea release. The combined experimental-machine learning model presented in this work emphasizes an efficient strategy for industrial waste valorization and enables data-driven optimization of hydrogel performance, suggesting significant potential for smart and sustainable agricultural applications.
