PT J
AU Chaabane, M
AF Chaabane, M.
TI Bridging microstructural evolution to macroscale dissipative behavior in carbon-black-filled elastomers via a physics-informed deep learning framework
SO EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF MECHANICS A-SOLIDS
LA English
DT Article
DE Filled rubbers; Physics-informed neural networks; Internal state variable theory; Viscoelastic-viscoplastic coupling; Stress softening
ID DAMAGE CONSTITUTIVE MODEL; TIME-DEPENDENT BEHAVIOR; RUBBER-LIKE MATERIALS; PERMANENT SET; DEFORMATION-BEHAVIOR; NETWORK ALTERATION; PART II; FORMULATION; HYSTERESIS; POLYMERS
AB This study presents, for the first time, a thermodynamically consistent, coupled viscoelastic-viscoplastic deep neural network model to capture and predict the time-dependent mechanical behavior of carbon-black-filled styrene-butadiene rubber (SBR) under cyclic loading. The proposed physics-informed deep learning (PIDL)based constitutive model adheres to fundamental thermodynamic principles, integrating the effects of stretch ratio and strain rate on cyclic fatigue behavior through two internal state variables. The first state variable governs recoverable network rearrangements, such as the movement of free chains within the relaxed network, viscous friction between fillers and matrix, and the breakdown and recovery of inter-aggregate linkages thereby influencing the viscoelastic response. The second variable accounts for unrecoverable network rearrangements, contributing to damage accumulation. By linking these internal state variables to the microstructural evolution of the rubber-filler system, the model directly connects microscopic phenomena to macroscopic mechanical behavior, enabling precise predictions of stress softening, hysteresis, dissipative heating, and free energy evolution. Additionally, it effectively captures the coupled effects of viscoelasticity and damage, offering deeper insights into the mechanisms driving the material's history-dependent cyclic response. This framework not only predicts the evolution of internal state variables but also clarifies how microstructural rearrangements govern macroscopic behavior, advancing the understanding of fatigue-induced phenomena in filled elastomers.
