TI Interpretable machine learning for optimizing piezoresistivity of carbon nanotube polymer nanocomposites for strain sensing
SO MATERIALS TODAY COMMUNICATIONS
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer; Nanocomposite; Carbon nanotubes; Piezoresistivity; Electrical Conductivity; Machine Learning
AB This study presents an interpretable machine learning (ML) framework for optimizing the piezoresistivity of carbon nanotube (CNT) polymer nanocomposites. A training dataset is generated using a stochastic multiscale numerical model. This dataset is used to train three ML models: random forest, extreme gradient boosting (XGBoost), and artificial neural networks. The dataset includes eight key design variables: CNT length, CNT diameter, number of CNT conduction channels, intrinsic CNT conductivity, CNT volume fraction, polymer energy barrier height, Poisson's ratio, and the applied strain, with the relative change in the resistance of the nano- composites as the output variable. Shapley additive explanations (SHAP) analysis is employed to uncover structure-property relationships guiding the optimization of the nanocomposites for improved strain-sensing sensitivity. This framework enables accelerated modeling of CNT/polymer nanocomposites aiding in the design of materials with optimized sensing properties for strain-sensing applications.
