TI Electrochemically synthesized multilayer metal-organic coordination polymer films for organic memristors with pattern recognition function
SO JOURNAL OF PHYSICS D-APPLIED PHYSICS
LA English
DT Article
DE metal-organic coordination polymer films; electrochemical synthesis; layer-by-layer structure; organic memristor; convolutional neural network; handwritten digit recognition
ID SYNAPSE
AB In recent years, metal-organic coordination polymer (MOCP) films have emerged as promising materials for memristor applications. However, the development of MOCP-based organic memristors remains challenging due to the complexities of synthesis, particularly for layer-by-layer (LbL) systems, which further increase the technological difficulty. In this study, we present a rapid and efficient electrochemical approach for synthesizing LbL MOCP films through a precisely controllable fabrication process. This method not only avoids the introduction of impurities but also enables precise thickness control by adjusting the reaction time, achieving a linear growth accuracy exceeding 90% for single-component films and multi-layered films. The fabricated multi-layered MOCP films exhibit good cycling endurance over 1000 V sweeps and an ON/OFF ratio of 54.4. Through continuous voltage sweeping, the multi-layered polymer-based memristor demonstrates 14 distinct linear conductance states. Moreover, a purely software-based convolutional neural network (CNN), incorporating the measured multistate conductance characteristics of the memristive devices in its simulation, achieves a high recognition accuracy of 98.5% for handwritten digits after training. These findings provide a promising pathway for the scalable fabrication of high-performance memristors with recognition functionality.
