TI A transferable residual fusion framework for rapid fatigue life prediction of short fiber reinforced polymer composites
SO COMPOSITES PART B-ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Article
DE Fiber reinforced polymer composite; Fatigue life prediction; Mechanism-guided neural network; Residual fusion framework; Transfer learning
AB Fatigue life prediction of fiber reinforced polymer (FRP) composites suffers from both costly testing and intrinsic data scatter. To improve the prediction accuracy, a transferable residual fusion framework (TRFF) featuring two coordinated neural networks (NN) is proposed, where a baseline NN maps loading parameters for an initial estimate while another residual NN captures the bias. TRFF provides high interpretability via a well-designed fusion architecture and enables rapid prediction by extracting failure features from merely the first 200 cycles, outperforming both standalone NNs and conventional regression methods. The framework is mechanismguided, rather than explicitly enforcing governing equations in the loss function. Guided by the experimental findings of short FRP (SFRP) that specimens with similar life exhibit similar cyclic responses and this invariance holds across temperatures, TRFF pretrained on ambient data has been successfully transferred to hightemperature conditions using a small dataset. Results show that utilizing a small calibration dataset of approximately 10% of the target data, equivalent to the amount required for a single traditional Wo & uml;hler curve, the transferred model reduces the root mean square error below 1.38 times the theoretical limit while maintaining over 85% of predictions within the 3 times error band. This work provides a rapid and interpretable solution for FRP fatigue evaluation under varying service environments.
