TI Tapered polymer optical microfiber sensor for noise-robust speech recognition
SO MEASUREMENT
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymer optical microfiber; Fiber-optic acoustic sensor; Speech recognition; Speaker identification; Noise-robust sensing
AB Speech recognition systems operating in noisy environments often suffer from performance degradation because airborne speech signals are easily contaminated by acoustic interference, and conventional electronic microphones may also be affected by electromagnetic disturbance. To address this issue, this study presents a non-contact optical acoustic measurement system based on a tapered polymer optical microfiber for robust detection of airborne speech signals under elevated background noise. Systematic characterization demonstrated a frequency response from 200 Hz to 3 kHz, linear sound-pressure sensitivity (R2 = 0.9952), and a signal-to-noise ratio of 60.2 dB. Frequency fidelity was evaluated using dual-tone multi-frequency signals, yielding a mean relative frequency error of 1.307% across repeated measurements. When integrated with a one-dimensional convolutional neural network classifier, the sensing system achieved 93.08% word recognition accuracy under 75 dB SPL noise, compared with 73.85% for a commercial smartphone microphone under identical conditions. Speaker identification across ten individuals reached 97.69% accuracy. These results demonstrate that the proposed polymer optical microfiber platform provides stable, linear, and repeatable acoustic measurement performance with improved robustness in noisy environments.
