TI Explaining the effect load levels have in fatigue compression after impact of carbon fiber reinforced polymers using convolutional neural network classification of acoustic waveforms
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF FATIGUE
LA English
DT Article
DE Composite materials; Delamination; Fatigue; Acoustic emissions; Convolutional neural network; Barely visible impact damage
ID LAMINATED COMPOSITES; BEHAVIOR; GROWTH
AB Fatigue damage propagation in impacted composites is still not fully understood, as some studies report steady delamination growth, while others show sudden, rapid growth at the end of fatigue life, even under similar test conditions. This study combines ultrasound scanning, digital image correlation, and acoustic emissions to characterize the fatigue-after-impact process across various load levels. Acoustic emissions were analyzed via wavelet transform and classified using a convolutional neural network (CNN) to isolate the contributions of individual damage modes throughout fatigue life. Results show that varying maximum compressive stress significantly influences compression after impact (CAI) fatigue behavior. Depending on the stress level, distinct damage propagation phases occur, each with different damage mode activity. Altering compressive stress affects not only the propagation rate and fatigue life but also the sequence of damage evolution. These findings offer a more complete phenomenological description of CAI fatigue and help resolve inconsistencies in previous studies.
