AU Nguyen, MH
   D'Mello, RJ
   Waas, AM
AF Nguyen, Minh Hoang
   D'Mello, Royan J.
   Waas, Anthony M.
TI Use of a neural network constitutive model for the size-dependent effects of curing on the deformation response and failure of fiber-reinforced polymer matrix composites
SO ARCHIVE OF APPLIED MECHANICS
LA English
DT Article
DE Fiber composites; Polymers; Size effect; Curing; Processing modeling; Residual stresses; Neural network surrogate
ID TENSILE-STRENGTH; KINETICS; STRESS; CURE
AB The mechanical performance of a thermoset composite material, which is subjected to temperature and pressure during fabrication, can be influenced by the details of the thermal and pressure cycles. During the curing process of a thermoset polymer, internal stresses can build up significantly. Thick structures coupled with the low conductivity of the polymer material may lead to a spatially varying temperature field that affects the property distribution and the residual stress field. In this paper, the cure-induced size effect on the stress development and tensile transverse failure response was investigated using a thermo-chemo-mechanical finite element-based framework, integrated with a crack band model. The present cure model takes into account the cure kinetics as well as the heat generation due to the chemical reaction and introduces a neural network-based constitutive model to capture the evolution of the matrix mechanical properties as a function of temperature and degree of cure. It was found that extremely thick composites developed more stress hot spots after curing due to a larger spatial gradient of the degree of cure, compared to thinner composites. However, thicker samples tend to achieve a more complete cure with fully developed strength properties, which may mitigate the presence of higher residual stresses. Yet premature failure in a thick composite may occur under shorter curing cycles due to incomplete cure.
