TI Finite-time neuro-modeling of complex reaction systems using robust exact differentiation: A data-driven application to non-ideal polymerization
SO APPLIED SOFT COMPUTING
LA English
DT Article
DE 2-vinylpyridine (2VP); 4-vinylpyridine (4VP); Kinetic modeling; Data-driven neural modeling; Robust exact differentiator; Finite-time differential neural network
ID KINETICS
AB This work presents a hybrid soft-computing framework for dynamic identification from sparse and noisy experi mental measurements. The method combines a finite-time differential neural network (FTDNN) with a Uniform Robust Exact Differentiator (URED) to reconstruct conversion trajectories, estimate reaction rates, and identify compact kinetic models. The framework was first evaluated using experimental free-radical polymerization data for 2-vinylpyridine (2VP) and 4-vinylpyridine (4VP) at 55, 65, and 75 degrees C. A synthetic database derived from these experiments was then used to assess reconstruction accuracy, noise attenuation, derivative estimation, sensitivity to initial conditions, and kinetic-parameter recovery under clean and noisy conditions. The FTDNN achieved an overall R2 of 0.9990 and reduced the reconstruction RMSE by 41.6% relative to the noisy input, while STA and URED clearly outperformed Euler differentiation in the presence of noise. The complete FTDNN-URED method also preserved accurate recovery of the nonlinear kinetic parameters. External evaluation was performed with independent acrylic-acid polymerization datasets covering non-ionized, partially ionized, and fully ionized condi tions. The proposed compact models reproduced these profiles with high accuracy and yielded a blind prediction with R2 = 0.9555 for an intermediate ionization condition excluded from parameter interpolation. These results indicate that the framework provides a consistent reconstruction and identification procedure within the noise levels, initial-condition perturbations, and polymerization systems evaluated in this study.
