TI Enhancing deep learning predictive models with HAPPY (Hierarchically Abstracted rePeat unit of PolYmers) representation
SO NPJ COMPUTATIONAL MATERIALS
LA English
DT Article
ID OPPORTUNITIES
AB We introduce HAPPY (Hierarchically Abstracted rePeat unit of PolYmers), a string representation for polymers, designed to efficiently encapsulate essential polymer structure features for property prediction. HAPPY assigns single constituent elements to groups of sub-structures and employs grammatically complete and independent connectors between chemical linkages. Using a limited number of datapoints, we trained neural networks utilizing both HAPPY and conventional SMILES encoding of repeated unit structures and compared their performance in predicting five polymer properties: dielectric constant, glass transition temperature, thermal conductivity, solubility, and density. The results showed that the HAPPY-based network could achieve higher prediction R-squared score and two-fold faster training times. We further tested the robustness and versatility of HAPPY-based network with an augmented training dataset. Additionally, we present topo-HAPPY (Topological HAPPY), an extension that incorporates topological details of the constituent connectivity, leading to improved solubility and glass transition temperature prediction R-squared score.
