TI Hybrid FEM-NN modeling of rate-dependent polymer mechanics across glass transition
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF MECHANICAL SCIENCES
LA English
DT Article
DE Polymers; Constitutive modeling; Finite element analysis; Viscoplastic; Neural network modeling; Glass transition temperature
ID CONSTITUTIVE MODEL; DEFORMATION-BEHAVIOR; NEURAL-NETWORKS; TEMPERATURE; STRAIN; POLYPROPYLENE; RANGE; FLOW
AB Developing constitutive models for polymers is challenging due to the coupled effects of strain rate and temperature. Conventional models often require separate calibration for each condition, limiting their predictive power, efficiency, and versatility. To address these challenges, this paper introduces a practical and powerful Neural Network (NN)-based constitutive model within a finite strain framework. The model effectively captures the mechanical behavior of polymers, particularly the strong coupling between strain rate and temperature, including the glass transition region. By training on athermal shear strength, a scalar parameter from a validated semi-crystalline model, our approach reduces the need for extensive experimental testing. Validation on both thermosets and thermoplastics confirms that the resulting hybrid model generalizes well to unseen loading conditions, ensuring broad applicability with minimal experimental input. Additionally, this method also allows for easy integration of Tension-Compression Asymmetry (TCA) and Glass Transition Sensitivity (GTS), customized for each polymer. The hybrid model was implemented as a user-defined material subroutine in finite element simulations of laboratory-scale specimens. Three-point bending and uniaxial tensile coupon tests, two of the most common initial experiments for assessing the suitability of a polymer typically performed under monotonic loading, were simulated to compare the behavior of the model with that of traditional constitutive models.
