TI Towards online control of molar mass distributions of non-linear polymers in emulsion polymerization using reinforcement learning
SO COMPUTERS & CHEMICAL ENGINEERING
LA English
DT Article
DE Machine learning; Reinforcement learning; Molar mass distribution; Emulsion polymerization; Online process control
ID MOLECULAR-WEIGHT DISTRIBUTION; POPULATION BALANCE-EQUATIONS; REACTION CALORIMETRY; COPOLYMERIZATION; DISCRETIZATION; REACTORS; SYSTEMS; MODELS
AB The incorporation of machine learning into polymer reaction engineering can allow for the optimization and control of complex reaction processes that have proven challenging to control using conventional methods. This work explores its use to aid in control of the molar mass distribution (MMD) of non-linear polymers synthesized by emulsion polymerization. The MMD is a key factor affecting polymer performance in industries like adhesives and paints, but controlling and optimizing it in non-linear polymers that contain crosslinked and/or branched chains is challenging due to several factors including limitations in online measurement techniques as well as the high complexity and computational expense of kinetic models of the process. Here, a simulated reactor system for semi-batch emulsion polymerization of butyl acrylate is used to learn a control policy using reinforcement learning (RL). The resulting control policy can be used to enable real-time decision-making and online control of the MMD by selecting key parameters, such as reactor temperature and monomer and initiator feeding rates. A significant development in the present work is the use of a surrogate neural network-based model for the simulated reactor system. It is shown that the surrogate model allows for training times to be significantly reduced as well as providing predictive capabilities, enabling operators to evaluate different control strategies and anticipate process outcomes in real-time.
