TI Design and longitudinal vibration control of a magnetorheological elastomer absorber based on Hammerstein model
SO INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF NON-LINEAR MECHANICS
LA English
DT Article
DE Magnetorheological elastomer absorber; Propulsion shafting; Neural network; Hammerstein model; Fuzzy control
ID FLUID
AB The pulsating forces generated by the propeller propagate through the propulsion shafting system to the hull, serving as a major contributor to ship vibration and noise. To mitigate the transmission of longitudinal vibration in marine propulsion shafts, a Magnetorheological Elastomer (MRE) absorber featuring broadband vibration control capability is introduced. To further address the hysteresis nonlinearity inherent in the MRE absorber and achieve accurate control of its output stiffness force, this study presents a fuzzy control strategy founded on a Hammerstein model. Within this framework, a BP neural network is utilized to model the nonlinear component of the Hammerstein structure, effectively capturing the hysteretic behavior of the MRE absorber, while a Linear Time-Invariant model characterizes its dynamic response. In contrast to conventional open-loop control approaches relying on inverse models, the proposed strategy substantially enhances the robustness of the MRE absorber system. The effectiveness of this control algorithm is demonstrated through simulations conducted on a propulsion shafting system integrated with the MRE absorber.
