TI All-screen-printed tactile sensor for robotic arm via polymeric thermal foaming
SO CHEMICAL ENGINEERING JOURNAL
LA English
DT Article
DE Flexible pressure sensor; Screen printing; Porous polymer; Closed-cell microstructure; Intelligent object recognition
ID PRESSURE SENSOR; SKIN
AB Intelligent tactile perception for robotic applications requires flexible pressure sensors with high linearity and wide detection range. Herein, we present a fully screen-printed pressure sensor with a porous polymer functional layer fabricated through thermal foaming technology. The sensor features a compact structure measuring 21.5 mm x 11.5 mm x 0.5 mm and weighing only 16.2 mg, with ultra-thin carbon-based electrodes printed on PET film. The closed-cell microstructure of the functional layer enables exceptional mechanical stability and signal linearity across a broad pressure range (0-2500 kPa) with a consistent sensitivity of 0.0034 kPa-1. The sensor demonstrates rapid response and recovery times of 41 ms and 92 ms respectively, and maintains exceptional stability after 10,000 high-intensity loading-unloading cycles at 150 kPa pressure. Additionally, the sensor exhibits minimal signal drift (within 0.03%) during prolonged operation for 1000 s in practical robotic gripping applications. When integrated with robotic grippers, the sensor enables intelligent object recognition through a back-propagation neural network (BPNN), achieving 97.78% accuracy in distinguishing different fruits based solely on tactile feedback. This cost-effective approach combining screen-printing technology with polymer foaming addresses key limitations in current tactile sensing systems and offers significant potential for industrial-scale implementation in industrial manipulators, smart agriculture, and self-driving labs.
