TI Cuprospinel decorated biopolymer functionalized CNFs based electrocatalytic platform integrated with grid search optimized neural network for detection of vital amino acid in beverages
SO FOOD CHEMISTRY
LA English
DT Article
DE Nanocomposite; Amino acid; Beverages; optimized neural network; Carbon nanofibers; Electrocatalysis
ID HETEROCYCLIC AROMATIC-AMINES; URIC-ACID; ELECTROCHEMICAL DETECTION; TRYPTOPHAN; COMPOSITE; NANOPARTICLES; GRAPHENE; SENSOR; OXIDE; NANOFIBERS
AB An electrocatalytic platform based on a novel nanocomposite integrated with a grid search-optimized neural network (GSNN) was proposed for intelligent sensing of tryptophan. The cuprospinel-decorated chitosan-functionalized carbon nanofibers (CuFe2O4/Chit-CNFs) fabricated on a disposable electrode revealed exceptional electrocatalytic activity with a low detection limit (2 nM) and good sensitivity (79.18 mu A mu M- 1 cm- 2) over a broad linear range (0.05-152.55 mu M). Cyclic voltammetry and differential pulse voltammetry were employed, and the sensing mechanism of tryptophan entails its electrocatalytic oxidation, where the synergistic impact of CuFe2O4 and Chit-CNFs boosts electrochemical response owing to their high surface area and conductivity. GSNN-based intelligent sensing returned a root mean square error (RMSE) of 2.76 and a mean absolute error (MAE) of 1.12. Moreover, the sensor's performance was tested on samples from apple juice, tomato juice, pineapple juice, and milk for assessing practicality, demonstrating recovery between 96.93 and 101.06 % and maximum relative standard deviation of 2.63 %. The proposed sensor showcased excellent selectivity, repeatability, reproducibility, and stability.
