TI Predicting the Flory-Huggins Parameter for Polymer-Solvents From Quantum Chemical Descriptors
SO JOURNAL OF POLYMER SCIENCE
LA English
DT Article
DE convolutional neural networks; Flory-Huggins parameters; QSPR; quantum chemical descriptors
ID ERROR
AB This study develops a predictive model for the Flory-Huggins interaction parameter (chi) using quantum chemical descriptors and accounting for temperature effects. A dataset of 2474 chi values across 19 polymers and 88 solvents was utilized. An optimized convolutional neural network (CNN) model, incorporating 30 selected features, demonstrated superior performance, achieving a mean absolute error (MAE) of 0.140, a root mean squared error (RMSE) of 0.177, and a coefficient of determination (R 2) of 0.973 on the test set. These results represent a significant improvement over existing quantitative structure-property relationship (QSPR) models (particularly for those with test set sizes exceeding 150 samples, which typically report RMSE > 0.25 and R-2 < 0.94). Mechanism analysis revealed that chi is primarily governed by charge-related properties (e.g., the most positive hydrogen charge in the polymer, H_p, and the most negative atomic charge in the solvent, N_s), polarity properties (polymer and solvent dipole moments, mu_p and mu_s), and solvent thermal energy (E_s). These factors collectively regulate the balance between specific molecular interactions and disorder effects. This study delivers not only a state-of-the-art predictive tool but also physical insight, establishing a new paradigm for the rational design of advanced polymer materials.
