TI Influence of surface modification on mechanical and tribology performance of jute filler polymer composites and prediction of the performance using artificial neural network
SO POLYMER BULLETIN
LA English
DT Article
DE Jute particulate; Wear; Sliding contact; Artificial neural network (ANN); Scanning electron microscopy
ID SLIDING WEAR BEHAVIOR; ABRASIVE WEAR; NATURAL FIBERS; REINFORCEMENT; FRICTION; GLASS
AB To meet the requirement of wear-resistant packaging for transportation and allied industries, an indigenously synthesized alkali-treated jute filler-reinforced epoxy composite is put to tribological tests for determining its worth/applicability under external loading. On the basis of full factorial design of experiments (DoE) at multiple filler percentages, sliding distances (1000, 2000, and 3000 m), and loads (5, 10, and 15 N), wear tests are conducted with the help of a pin-on-disk multitribometer machine. The mechanical characterizations like hardness and tensile strength of the composites are also evaluated, and the results of experimented tensile strength are validated with the results of Digimat-FE tool. Among all the different jute filler wt.% (0%, 2.5%, 5%, 7.5%, 10% and 12.5%)-reinforced composite, 2.5 and 5 wt% filler-reinforced composites have produced better wear resistance properties and maximum tensile strength, respectively. On the other hand, agglomeration of fillers on the surface at higher jute filler wt% (= 12.5%) leads to a high amount of plastic deformation and debonding. The observations reported in the present study are supported by a single hidden layer artificial neural network (SHL-ANN) model with the error being as low as +/- 2% when compared with linear regression model for different tribological properties.
