DT Article
DE Glass transition temperature; Graph neural networks; Inductive bias
AB The accurate prediction of the glass transition temperature ( T- g ) is crucial for polymer design. Standard Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) exhibit a systematic bias when applied to this task: their homogeneous graph readout mechanism fails to distinguish the distinct functional roles of backbone and side-chain atoms, leading to structural role mixing and a consequent overestimation error that amplifies in the high-T g regime. To address this, we propose a novel role-decoupled graph readout paradigm. Our framework, termed BRAG, explicitly separates the learning paths for backbone and side-chain atoms based on molecular topology, and then models their competitive contribution to segmental mobility via a physics-informed absolute-difference operation. This design injects a structure-inductive bias directly into the representation learning process. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method not only achieves comparable overall accuracy but, more distinctively, effectively rebalances the prediction error distribution. It reduces the high-T g region error (up to 6.3% relative to the standard GNN baseline and mitigates the systematic bias prevalent in standard GNNs, p < 0.05 ) and substantially mitigates the high-T( g )systematic bias that plagues standard GNNs. Ablation studies confirm the necessity of both role-decoupling and the differential interaction. This work highlights that for materials with intrinsic functional heterogeneity, architectural alignment with domain knowledge is a more effective lever for achieving model robustness and physical consistency than increasing model complexity.
