TI Performance and lifespan prediction of pultruded glass fibre reinforced polymer composite cross-arms in transmission towers: A review of machine learning approaches
SO STRUCTURES
LA English
DT Review
DE pGFRP composites; Creep; Stacking Sequence; Finite Element Analysis; Machine Learning
ID MECHANICAL-PROPERTIES; ORIENTATION; BEHAVIOR; CREEP
AB Pultruded glass fibre-reinforced polymer (pGFRP) composite cross-arms are increasingly adopted in transmission towers due to their lightweight, non-conductive properties and potential 75-year service life, yet accurately predicting their long-term creep lifespan remains challenging due to complex nonlinear interactions among stacking sequences, environmental exposure, and sustained mechanical loading. This systematic review of 128 studies evaluates machine learning (ML) applications, including gene expression programming (GEP), neural networks, and ensemble methods, for pGFRP composite creep prediction and benchmarks performance against traditional physics-based models such as Findley's power law and the Burgers model. ML demonstrates superior predictive accuracy with 5.2% error vs 12-18% for empirical models when modelling optimised (+/- 45 degrees/0 degrees/90 degrees) stacking sequences that reduce time-dependent strain by 10-23%, validated by finite element analysis (FEA) with less than 1% error, with pGFRP composite showing 32% less deflection and 34% higher creep strength versus Balau timber under operational conditions. However, four critical limitations currently prevent reliable 50-year lifespan predictions, which is data scarcity as less than 500 creep hours limits training robustness, poor climate transferability, such in tropical datasets fail in temperate freeze-thaw conditions, unaddressed scale effects as coupon-level properties don't translate to full-scale cross-arm behavior, and lack of long-term field monitoring as more than 2 years continuous exposure data unavailable. The review proposes an integrated MLphysics framework combining accelerated creep testing, FEA validation, stacking optimisation, and data-driven extrapolation to bridge these experimental limitations and address research gaps, ultimately enabling robust, climate-agnostic lifespan predictions essential for the deployment of global pGFRP composite in transmission infrastructure.
