TI Exploring SMILES and BigSMILES: Toward Efficient and Scalable Polymer Representations for Machine Learning
SO MACROMOLECULES
LA English
DT Article
ID LINE NOTATION; LANGUAGE
AB A simple yet versatile polymer representation serves as a cornerstone for the large-scale adoption of polymer machine learning (ML), especially in the regime of large language models (LLMs). Among emerging approaches, BigSMILES stands out as an elegantly crafted representation, designed with a refined syntax to transcend the limitations of SMILES, most notably its struggle to capture the stochastic nature of polymers. However, in practical polymer ML tasks, the performance comparison between BigSMILES-based and SMILES-based ML models remains an open question, posing a challenge to the further development and adoption of advanced polymer representations. In this study, we systematically evaluate SMILES and BigSMILES across 12 polymer-related tasks, including forward property prediction and reverse design, using convolutional neural networks and LLMs. Our results not only show that BigSMILES achieves comparable or superior performance to SMILES, but also first demonstrate its ability to accelerate LLMs training due to its reduced token complexity. This finding is anticipated to reduce energy and cost expenditures in the training of polymer LLMs, while also facilitating the expedited development of more advanced polymer LLM agents, as we have further demonstrated that BigSMILES can more accurately encode chemical information and monomer connectivity for copolymers. This work serves as a starting point for highlighting the potential of BigSMILES to streamline and accelerate polymer ML workflows. Looking ahead, advancing polymer representations to integrate polymer chain structure, phase morphology, and processing parameters will be crucial for capturing the multifaceted relationships between polymer structure and properties, driving more accurate, efficient, and scalable modeling.
